BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:History Talks - Three Pioneers
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T113000
UID:260623Jr_tBD5MSkmntSKkxAP-Sg
DESCRIPTION:**Carrillo-Fitch\, Heald\, Matheson**\n\nClaire O’Neill\, Ni
 ta Miller\, Don Anderson\n\nLearn about three Healdsburg pioneers\, all of
  whom shaped Healdsburg’s history in of the Gold Rush. Claire O’Neill 
 will introduce you to Josefa Carrillo Fitch\, from a San Diego Spanish fam
 ily\, who arrived after her husband’s death and discovered that squatter
 s were inhabiting much of the land her family owned as the Rancho Sotoyome
  land grant.\n\nNita Miller will talk about Harmon Heald\, one of those sq
 uatters and a failed gold seeker from Ohio. He eventually created a subdiv
 ision on land he purchased from the Fitch family and that became the cente
 r of the town named for him.\n\nAnd Don Anderson will present the life of 
 Roderick Matheson\, a Scotsman\, who found his way to Healdsburg where he 
 was a civic leader\, and then met his death during the Civil War after acc
 epting a commission from Lincoln.
LOCATION:Healdsburg Plaza Gazebo
URL:https://www.healdsburgmuseum.org/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:"The Water Baby" read live at Jack London State Historic Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260711T190000
UID:260622KHQyEVg0bkOkiMLAJg3QPA
DESCRIPTION:Storyteller Jeff Falconer will present a live reading of Jack 
 London’s short story “The Water Baby” in the historic cottage garden
 .\n\nPublished in London’s Hawaiian story collection “On the Makaloa M
 at\,” this is a reflective tale that explores childhood\, imagination\, 
 and humanity’s connection to the natural world. Set against a backdrop o
 f water and wonder\, the story offers a glimpse into a lesser-known side o
 f London’s writing.\n\nGuests are invited to gather beside the garden po
 nd and experience the story as it was meant to be shared—through the spo
 ken word. Following the reading\, participants will have an opportunity to
  discuss the story’s themes and place within Jack London’s body of wor
 k.\n\nThis intimate program is part of the year-long celebration of Jack L
 ondon’s 150th birthday and offers a unique chance to engage with one of 
 his lesser-known short stories.\n\nInterested in reading along? Copies of 
 “On the Makaloa Mat\,” the collection containing Jack London’s short
  story “The Water Baby\,” are available for purchase. Visit the park
 ’s Museum Bookstore or order a copy at https://jacklondonpark.com/produc
 t/on-the-makaloa-mat/.
LOCATION:Jack London State Historic Park
URL:https://jacklondonpark.com/events/read-aloud-the-water-baby/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
