BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Santa Rosa Wednesday Night Market
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260722T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260722T000000
UID:260526mRf7mcehWkqwryxS_u-FNw
DESCRIPTION:The Santa Rosa Wednesday Night Market is a beloved free commun
 ity event that's been a tradition for 37 years and brings people together 
 to celebrate local culture\, small businesses\, and the vibrant spirit of 
 downtown. Our mission is to create an inclusive\, welcoming public space w
 here residents and visitors can gather to enjoy great food\, live entertai
 nment\, and unique local vendors while supporting the economic and social 
 health of our city. This lively open-air street fair features a curated se
 lection of local artisans\, farmers\, food vendors\, and community organiz
 ations. Guests can enjoy live music and performances\, explore handmade go
 ods\, discover fresh local products\, and connect with the people who make
  Sonoma County special. Families are especially welcome. The Market includ
 es a dedicated Kids Zone with free activities for children\, interactive e
 xperiences\, and opportunities for learning and play. Local nonprofit orga
 nizations also participate each week\, sharing information about their pro
 grams and helping strengthen connections across the community. The Wednesd
 ay Night Market is more than an event-it's a celebration of community\, cr
 eativity\, and the local businesses and organizations that help Santa Rosa
  thrive.
LOCATION:Old Courthouse Square
URL:https://www.visitsantarosa.com/events/#jlupcoming-events
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Feminist Book Club: Sister Outsider: Essays and Speeches by Audre 
 Lorde
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260722T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260722T183000
UID:260709zuD2rOl9jUW4rNcNgBZY_g
DESCRIPTION:The Feminist Book Club meets monthly to discuss books explorin
 g feminist issues. Selections for the club span literature\, memoir\, grap
 hic novels\, essay collections and works of nonfiction. Hosted by Jenna Sa
 nders and Stephanie Der\, the Feminist Book Club is open to people and the
 ir experiences from multiple genders and races. Register to receive a link
  to the event one week prior. ABOUT THE BOOK Presenting the essential writ
 ings of black lesbian poet and feminist writer Audre Lorde\, Sister Outsid
 er celebrates an influential voice in twentieth-century literature. “\\[
 Lorde's\\] works will be important to those truly interested in growing up
  sensitive\, intelligent\, and aware.” — The New York Times In this ch
 arged collection of fifteen essays and speeches\, Lorde takes on sexism\, 
 racism\, ageism\, homophobia\, and class\, and propounds social difference
  as a vehicle for action and change. Her prose is incisive\, unflinching\,
  and lyrical\, reflecting struggle but ultimately offering messages of hop
 e. This commemorative edition includes a new foreword by Lorde-scholar and
  poet Cheryl Clarke\, who celebrates the ways in which Lorde's philosophie
 s resonate more than twenty years after they were first published. These l
 andmark writings are\, in Lorde's own words\, a call to “never close our
  eyes to the terror\, to the chaos which is Black which is creative which 
 is female which is dark which is rejected which is messy which is . . . 
 ” ABOUT THE AUTHOR A writer\, activist\, and mother of two\, Audre Lorde
  grew up in 1930s Harlem. She earned a master’s degree in library scienc
 e from Columbia University\, received a National Endowment for the Arts gr
 ant for poetry\, and was New York State’s Poet Laureate from 1991 to 199
 3. She is the author of twelve books\, including Zami and The Black Unicor
 n. Lorde died of cancer at the age of fifty-eight in 1992.
URL:https://www.facebook.com/events/2232110170863929/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
